        {
          "english": "En-jemusi (Ilchamus) Cosmology (En-Gai & En-Gop) and Customary Law Recorded by Mervyn W. H. Beech (1911, pp. 4, 19, 27–37)",
          "translation": "Recorded from the En-jemusi warrior Launjali at Lake Baringo (Beech 1911, pp. 4, 19 fn. 1, 27–37): Cosmology (p. 19 fn. 1): Two benevolent deities — En-Gai (God of the Sky) and En-Gop (God of the Earth) — while the Sun and Moon are husband and wife • Oaths (pp. 28–29): (1) Drinking goat's neck-blood mixed with milk, (2) Stepping over two spears placed point-to-point, (3) Throwing blood drawn from disputed cattle's necks onto accuser, accused, and cattle • Customary Law & Governance (pp. 29–37): Governed by a supreme hereditary chief with a council of elders; Murder fine = all property (or compromise of 10–20 cows from murderer + 2–6 cows from each relative; unintentional homicide = 10 cows + 20 goats); Theft = beating on 1st offence, 3x value on 2nd offence ('if 3 thieves steal 1 cow, each pays 3 cows: 9 in all'); Bride-price = 6 goats to bride's father; Land Tenure (p. 14): Allotments marked out by the chief (as much land per wife as one woman can till), inherited by sons only, while irrigation ditches belong to the tribe and are maintained exclusively by the old men",
          "pronunciation": "ehn-gah-ee • ehn-gohp • lah-oon-jah-lee",
          "context": "Mervyn W. H. Beech (1911) 'The Suk: Their Language and Folklore', pp. 4, 19 fn. 1, 27–37",
          "audioFile": null
        },
        {
          "english": "Sir Harry Johnston's (1902, Vol. II, pp. 798–802, 810–813, 835) Account of the Enjámusi (Nyarusi) Agricultural Masai of Lake Baringo",
          "translation": "Enjámusi / Nyarusi ('wizards', p. 798 fn. 3): agricultural Masai settled at the south end of Lake Baringo alongside the Gwas' Ngishu (at Eldama Ravine) and Burkeneji • Architecture & economy (pp. 808–813, 835, Figs. 441, 450, 451, 463): permanent villages surrounded by strong palisades with doors of huge hewn planks, round wattle-and-daub huts with conical grass-thatched roofs, raised mud-and-dung-plastered granaries for corn, pumpkins, and beans, keeping humped zebu cattle, sheep, goats, and fowls, catching fish by trapping and spearing, basket-weaving, mat-making, and operating open-air charcoal iron forges with twin bellows (Fig. 463)",
          "pronunciation": "en-ja-mu-si / nya-ru-si / gwa-so en-gi-shu",
          "context": "Sir Harry H. Johnston (1902) 'The Uganda Protectorate, Vol. II', Ch. XIX, pp. 798–802, 810–813, 835",
